--TEST--
Core\Time::fromIso accepts only a text that carries its own offset and refuses the rest as a ParseError under its own name — spec §§ 4, 10
--FILE--
<?mwl
// § 4 gives `fromIso` the half of `strtotime` that reads an ISO-8601
// timestamp, so what it needs is an *absolute* point and the text has to
// carry its own offset. A civil time with none is `Core\Time::parse`'s
// question, which takes a zone as a third argument in order to answer it,
// and a relative expression is not accepted in either half.
//
// Counted over a table rather than read off a line: fourteen texts, of which
// exactly the five spelling one absolute point are accepted — and they spell
// the *same* point, so the epoch second they answer is one value rather than
// five separately plausible ones.
var $reference = Core\Time::fromIso("2024-03-01T12:00:00Z")->toEpochSeconds();
array<string> $texts = [
    // Accepted: the offset written as `Z`, as either sign, with a subsecond,
    // and with a space where the `T` goes — five spellings, one point.
    "2024-03-01T12:00:00Z",
    "2024-03-01T14:00:00+02:00",
    "2024-03-01T07:00:00-05:00",
    "2024-03-01T12:00:00.500Z",
    "2024-03-01 12:00:00Z",
    // Refused for a missing component: the offset, the time, the date.
    "2024-03-01T12:00:00",
    "2024-03-01",
    "12:00:00Z",
    // Refused as a relative expression, which `strtotime` reads and this
    // member does not, and the empty text.
    "now",
    "+1 day",
    "",
    // Refused as a calendar value that no year holds.
    "2024-02-30T12:00:00Z",
    "2024-13-01T12:00:00Z",
    "2024-03-01T25:00:00Z",
];
int $accepted = 0;
int $sameSecond = 0;
int $refused = 0;
int $named = 0;
foreach ($texts as string $text) {
    try {
        var $at = Core\Time::fromIso($text);
        $accepted = $accepted + 1;
        if ($at->toEpochSeconds() == $reference) {
            $sameSecond = $sameSecond + 1;
        }
    } catch (ParseError $bad) {
        // The class is § 10's `ParseError` and not a bare `RuntimeError`:
        // this member's one argument is text that arrived from elsewhere,
        // which is the same rule `Core\Time::parse` splits its two classes
        // by. A wrong class would not be caught here and would end the case.
        $refused = $refused + 1;
        if (Core\Str::startsWith($bad->message, 'Core\Time::fromIso(): ')) {
            $named = $named + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $accepted, " accepted, ", $sameSecond, " of them at one second\n";
echo $refused, " refused, ", $named, " under the member's own name\n";

// The sentence past that name is `jiff`'s, because it says which component
// it stopped at and nothing here would say it better — so it is asserted by
// its class and by the name in front of it rather than frozen.

// The bound, named on both sides, and it is one character wide: the same
// civil time is refused without an offset and accepted with one.
try {
    echo Core\Time::fromIso("2024-03-01T12:00:00")->toIso(), "\n";
} catch (ParseError $civil) {
    echo "no offset: refused\n";
}
echo "with one: ", Core\Time::fromIso("2024-03-01T12:00:00Z")->toIso(), "\n";

// The five accepted texts share a second, not a point: a subsecond is read
// and kept, and `toEpochSeconds` is what truncates it.
echo "subsecond: ", Core\Time::fromIso("2024-03-01T12:00:00.500Z")->toIso(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
5 accepted, 5 of them at one second
9 refused, 9 under the member's own name
no offset: refused
with one: 2024-03-01T12:00:00Z
subsecond: 2024-03-01T12:00:00.5Z
